{"qid":"Q33","names":["Republic of Finland"],"title":"Republic of Finland","endonym":{"native":"Finland","lang":"sv"},"capsule":"Finland declared independence from Russia on 6 December 1917, following the collapse of tsarist authority and a brief, bloody civil war in early 1918 between 'White' government forces and 'Red' socialist militias that left approximately 36,000 dead. The country resisted Soviet invasion in the Winter War (1939–1940), ceding roughly eleven percent of its territory under the Moscow Peace Treaty, then fought alongside Germany in the Continuation War (1941–1944) to recover lost lands before concluding a separate armistice. Post-war Finland navigated a distinctive policy of neutrality and managed accommodation with the Soviet Union — 'Finlandization' in contemporary parlance — while developing into one of the world's highest-income welfare states. Finland joined the European Union in 1995 and NATO in April 2023.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Finland's international boundaries since 1947 are precisely defined by the Paris Peace Treaty and fully attested; the pre-1940 boundary with the USSR in Karelia is historically documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Finland","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Finland"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Finland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finland"}],"britannica":"place/Finland","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Finland","rulers":[{"qid":"Q440166","name":"Lauri Ingman","start":1918,"end":1919,"image":"Ingman Lauri.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Ingman%20Lauri.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{